TI  - Two years of explicit CiTO annotations.
PG  - 14
LID - 10.1186/s13321-023-00683-2 [doi]
LID - 14
AB  - Citations are an essential aspect of research communication and have become the 
      basis of many evaluation metrics in the academic world. Some see citation counts 
      as a mark of scientific impact or even quality, but in reality the reasons for 
      citing other work are manifold which makes the interpretation more complicated 
      than a single citation count can reflect. Two years ago, the Journal of 
      Cheminformatics proposed the CiTO Pilot for the adoption of a practice of 
      annotating citations with their citation intentions. Basically, when you cite a 
      journal article or dataset (or any other source), you also explain why 
      specifically you cite that source. Particularly, the agreement and disagreement 
      and reuse of methods and data are of interest. This article explores what 
      happened after the launch of the pilot. We summarize how authors in the Journal 
      of Cheminformatics used the pilot, shows citation annotations are distributed 
      with Wikidata, visualized with Scholia, discusses adoption outside BMC, and 
      finally present some thoughts on what needs to happen next.
