TI  - Context-dependent similarity analysis of analogue series for structure-activity 
      relationship transfer based on a concept from natural language processing.
PG  - 5
LID - 10.1186/s13321-025-00951-3 [doi]
LID - 5
AB  - Analogue series (AS) are generated during compound optimization in medicinal 
      chemistry and are the major source of structure-activity relationship (SAR) 
      information. Pairs of active AS consisting of compounds with corresponding 
      substituents and comparable potency progression represent SAR transfer events for 
      the same target or across different targets. We report a new computational 
      approach to systematically search for SAR transfer series that combines an AS 
      alignment algorithm with context-depending similarity assessment based on vector 
      embeddings adapted from natural language processing. The methodology 
      comprehensively accounts for substituent similarity, identifies non-classical 
      bioisosteres, captures substituent-property relationships, and generates accurate 
      AS alignments. Context-dependent similarity assessment is conceptually novel in 
      computational medicinal chemistry and should also be of interest for other 
      applications.Scientific contributionA method is reported to systematically search 
      for and align analogue series with SAR transfer potential. Central to the 
      approach is the assessment of context-dependent similarity for substituents, a 
      new concept in cheminformatics, which is based upon vector embeddings and word 
      pair relationships adapted from natural language processing.
